<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/lib/Snippet.abide'
import Widget from './lib/Widget.abide'
import widgetSource from './lib/Widget.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import layoutSource from '../../layout.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="`csp()` lets your own build through, and nothing else">
        <Example
            id="csp-blocked"
            title="`csp()` — your own scripts run, a stranger's do not"
            summary="A support widget somebody pasted into the CRM from another origin. Add it, and the policy this page was served with refuses it."
            files={{
                'lib/Widget.abide': widgetSource,
                'src/layout.abide': layoutSource,
            }}
        >
            <Widget />
        </Example>

        <p>The rung is <code>csp()</code> in the root layout, and it is the policy every page of
        these docs is served under. Every script and stylesheet on the page ran, because the build
        serves them from its own origin. The widget's address is another origin, so <code>script-src
        'self'</code> refused it before a request was made. The inline script the render writes is
        let through by a hash taken at build time, which is why <code>'unsafe-inline'</code> never
        has to appear.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Adding a source, not writing a policy">
        <Snippet source={layoutSource} path="src/layout.abide" from={['middleware(csp(']} />

        <p>These docs name one directive. They frame their own invoice previews, so
        <code>frame-ancestors</code> is <code>'self'</code> where the baseline says
        <code>'none'</code>, and every other directive is the baseline's.</p>

        <p>Each directive you name <strong>replaces</strong> that directive, and only that one:
        naming <code>img-src</code> because your avatars live on a CDN does not require you to also
        know what <code>script-src</code> should say for a bundle you did not hand-write. The one
        you name is the whole directive, so it says <code>'self'</code> again. A directive given an
        empty array is dropped, and one the baseline lacks is added.</p>

        <p>That is the shape worth insisting on: a policy an app maintains by hand goes stale the
        first time the build changes, and it goes stale silently — the symptom is a blocked script
        in a browser nobody was testing in.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`csp.nonce` is this response's nonce">
        <Code language="abide" caption="src/layout.abide — excerpt" code={`<script>
import { csp } from '@abide/abide'
</script>

<head>
    <script nonce={csp.nonce()}>window.dataLayer = []</script>
</head>`} />

        <p>A nonce is per response, which is what makes it a nonce. <code>csp.nonce()</code> hands
        back the one this response is carrying, so an inline script the app genuinely wants is
        allowed without <code>'unsafe-inline'</code> — and <code>'unsafe-inline'</code> is the
        directive that turns a policy into a comment.</p>

        <p>Reading it is the only way to get it. There is no configuration that stamps a nonce onto
        markup, because a nonce on markup the app did not write is a nonce on markup nobody vouched
        for.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/set-the-title-and-social-preview')}>Head &amp;
        metadata</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`nosniff` is separate, and unconditional">
        <p><code>x-content-type-options: nosniff</code> is on <strong>every</strong> abide response,
        unconditionally, and is not something <code>csp()</code> turns on. Every response declares
        its own content type, so a browser guessing a different one is only ever the vulnerability —
        a JSON refusal sniffed as HTML is script running on your origin.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/answer-with-something-other-than-json')}>Response
        types</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="No page can be framed by default">
        <p>Every page carries <code>content-security-policy: frame-ancestors 'none'</code> before
        any rung runs, so another site cannot put your page in an invisible frame and steer a click
        onto it. <code>csp()</code> replaces that header with the whole policy, whose baseline says
        the same thing — so opening the page to a frame is one directive:
        <code>{'csp({ \'frame-ancestors\': ["\'self\'"] })'}</code>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`csp()` is a rung, and composes with the others">
        <p><code>csp()</code> hands back a <code>Middleware</code>, so it sits in
        <code>middleware(...)</code> alongside whatever else runs per request, in registration
        order. It belongs on the <strong>app</strong> lane rather than a handler's: pre-routing is
        where a response header that is about the document goes.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/run-code-at-start-and-stop')}>Lifecycle</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/app/run-code-at-start-and-stop')}>Lifecycle</a> — where the rung is
            registered</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> — the lane it
            runs on</li>
            <li><a href={url('/reference/helpers')}>Helpers</a> — <code>csp</code> beside
            <code>log</code>, <code>url</code> and <code>navigate</code></li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
